= 1
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

For <two equal lenses with a virtual intermediate object>, use positive distances for real objects on a lens's incident side and <real images> on its outgoing side. The first lens gives $1/v_1=1/f-1/u_1=1/30-1/60$, hence $v_1=60\,\mathrm{cm}$. At the second lens these rays are already converging toward a point $50\,\mathrm{cm}$ to its right: its object distance is therefore $u_2=-50\,\mathrm{cm}$.
$$
\frac1{v_2}=\frac1{30}-\frac1{-50}=\frac4{75}\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}},\qquad
\boxed{v_2=18.75\,\mathrm{cm}\text{ to the right of lens 2}.}
$$
Thus **the final image is real**, $28.75\,\mathrm{cm}$ to the right of lens 1. Its <transverse magnification> is $(-60/60)(-18.75/[-50])=-3/8$: it is inverted. The intermediate focus is not a real object placed before lens 2.

\Image[paper-1-ia2010-lenses.png]
{title=Two converging lenses and a real image}
{description=Original paraxial ray construction with an arbitrary small object height. Two rays meet at the calculated inverted final image; dashed continuations show where lens 1 alone would have focused them.}

= 2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

For <prism sweep under different rotation conventions>, let the apex angle be $A=60^\circ$. Snell's law at both faces gives
$$
r_1=\arcsin(\sin i/1.5),\qquad r_2=A-r_1,\qquad e=\arcsin(1.5\sin r_2),\qquad D=i+e-A.
$$
Here $e$ is measured from the second face's outward normal, whereas $D$ is the deviation from the incident direction. The two settings give
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc}
i&e&D\\\hline
50^\circ&47.2093^\circ&37.2093^\circ\\
60^\circ&38.8768^\circ&38.8768^\circ
\end{array}
$$
For a fixed laser and rotating prism, the outgoing beam therefore sweeps
$$
\boxed{|\Delta D|=1.6675^\circ\approx1.67^\circ.}
$$
If instead the prism stays fixed and the input beam is steered, its exit-face normal stays fixed and the answer is **$|\Delta e|=8.3325^\circ$**. The source does not explicitly say which component moves, so the laboratory-angle convention matters. Both settings transmit through the second face; total internal reflection does not occur.

= 3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Apply <Ampère's law> on a concentric circle. Uniform <current density> is $J=I/(\pi a^2)$, so the enclosed current is $Ir^2/a^2$ inside and $I$ outside. With permeability $\mu_0$,
$$
\boxed{B(r)=\begin{cases}\mu_0Ir/(2\pi a^2),&0\le r\le a,\\\mu_0I/(2\pi r),&r\ge a.\end{cases}}
$$
The field circulates azimuthally by the <right-hand rule>. Its magnitude rises linearly from zero, is continuous and maximal at the surface, then falls as $1/r$. End effects are neglected.

\Image[paper-1-ia2010-rod-field.png]
{title=Magnetic field of a uniform-current rod}
{description=Original dimensionless Ampere-law field curve: B divided by its surface value versus radius divided by rod radius, with the surface and the change of radial slope marked.}

= 4
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

In SI base dimensions,
$$
[e]=IT,\quad[\epsilon_0]=I^2T^4M^{-1}L^{-3},\quad[m]=M,\quad[h]=ML^2T^{-1}.
$$
Equating current, time, <mass> and <length> powers respectively yields
$$
\alpha+2\beta=0,\quad\alpha+4\beta-\delta=0,\quad-\beta+\gamma+\delta=0,\quad-3\beta+2\delta=1.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\alpha=-2,\quad\beta=1,\quad\gamma=-1,\quad\delta=2,\qquad a_0\propto\frac{\epsilon_0h^2}{me^2}.}
$$
<Dimensional analysis> cannot determine the dimensionless proportionality constant.

= 5
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For a <proper lifetime inferred from a delayed decay flash>, the measured interval ends when the light returns to the detector, not when the <muon> decays. Put passage at $(t,x)=(0,0)$ in the detector frame. If decay occurs at $t_d$, its distance is $vt_d$, and the backwards <photon> takes another $vt_d/c$ to arrive. Therefore
$$
t_{\rm record}=t_d(1+v/c),\qquad t_d=\frac{10\,\mathrm{ns}}{1+1/2}=\frac{20}{3}\,\mathrm{ns}.
$$
The <muon>'s proper elapsed time is
$$
\boxed{\Delta\tau=t_d\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}
=10\,\mathrm{ns}\sqrt{\frac{1-v/c}{1+v/c}}
=\frac{10}{\sqrt3}\,\mathrm{ns}\approx5.77\,\mathrm{ns}.}
$$
Applying <time dilation> directly to the 10 ns reception interval would transform the wrong pair of events.

= 6
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

For <background-subtracted radiative temperature uncertainty>, assume the two quoted precisions are independent <standard uncertainties>, and treat the radiative proportionality factor as known. Write $P_B=B$ and $P_T=10B$. Then $P_S=9B$, while both absolute input uncertainties equal $0.5B$. Consequently
$$
\sigma_S=\sqrt{(0.05\times10B)^2+(0.50B)^2}=\frac{B}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\frac{\sigma_T}{T}=\frac14\frac{\sigma_S}{P_S}.
$$
Thus **the <temperature> precision is about $1.96\%$, or $2\%$**. Subtraction amplifies fractional uncertainty even though $P_T$ itself is precise.

Independence is a measurement assumption, not a consequence of the two percentages. For <correlation> coefficient $r_c$, the result is $\sigma_T/T=\sqrt{2(1-r_c)}/72$. If the percentages are strict error bounds combined pessimistically, the corresponding bound is $2.78\%$. Unknown calibration <correlations> cannot be inferred from the source.

= Section B
{c}
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= B7
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B7}

For the <edge-hinged cube with a central spherical cavity>, use the parallel-axis theorem: for a parallel axis at perpendicular distance $d$ from the centre-of-mass axis, $I=I_C+Md^2$. Let $M=\rho a^3$. The centre lies $d=a/\sqrt2$ from the edge hinge, so
$$
\boxed{I_h=Ma^2/6+M(a/\sqrt2)^2=\frac23Ma^2=\frac23\rho a^5.}
$$
At equilibrium the centre is vertically below the hinge. Its gravitational <torque> at angular displacement $\theta$ is $-Mgd\sin\theta$. Therefore the small-angle equation is $I_h\ddot\theta+Mgd\theta=0$, with
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=\frac{Mgd}{I_h}=\frac{3g}{2\sqrt2a}.}
$$
The removed <sphere> has <mass> $M_s=\pi M/6$ and the same centre. Its hinge inertia is
$$
I_{s,h}=M_s\left[\frac25(a/2)^2+(a/\sqrt2)^2\right]=\frac35M_sa^2=\frac\pi{10}Ma^2.
$$
<Superposition> leaves the centre at the same location but changes both inertia and weight:
$$
\boxed{I_{\rm hollow}=Ma^2\left(\frac23-\frac\pi{10}\right),\qquad
\Omega=\omega\sqrt{\frac{1-\pi/6}{1-3\pi/20}}\approx0.9492\omega.}
$$
The <frequency> decreases: the removed central material contributes less hinge inertia per unit <mass> than the original cube average.

= B8
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B8}

Integrating coaxial cylindrical shells gives
$$
I=\int_0^a r^2\frac{2m r}{a^2}\,dr=\frac12ma^2.
$$
<Rolling without slipping> has spin <speed> $u/a$, so **$K=mu^2/2+Iu^2/(2a^2)=3mu^2/4$**.

For the <rolling-cylinder loop contact threshold>, set $\ell=R-a$, the <radius> of the centre's circular path, and let $\theta$ run from the loop bottom. The centre starts $h$ above its lowest centre position. Its height gain from the bottom is $\ell(1-\cos\theta)$. <Energy> conservation gives
$$
u^2=\frac43g[h-\ell(1-\cos\theta)].
$$
At the top, contact requires $N=m(u_t^2/\ell-g)\ge0$, hence $u_t^2\ge g\ell$. Inserting the <energy> relation proves the limiting condition
$$
\boxed{h\ge\frac{11}{4}\ell=\frac{11(R-a)}4.}
$$
The same condition keeps $N\ge0$ elsewhere on the circle, since
$$
\frac{N}{mg}=\frac43(h/\ell-1)+\frac73\cos\theta
$$
is smallest at the top.

For the centre's <acceleration>, the inward normal component is $u^2/\ell$ and the tangential component is $-(2g/3)\sin\theta$. If $A=h/\ell-1\ge7/4$ and $x=\cos\theta$,
$$
|\mathbf a_C|^2=\frac{4g^2}{9}[4A^2+8Ax+3x^2+1].
$$
This convex quadratic has its largest endpoint value at $x=1$. **The maximum on the circular loop is at the bottom, directed vertically upward.** For the limiting release height,
$$
\boxed{a_{C,\max}=u_b^2/\ell=\frac{11g}{3}\approx36.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}.}
$$
The approach track's curvature is unspecified, so a global bound for an arbitrary approach shape cannot be proved from the sketch alone; the result above concerns the specified circular part and the usual smooth approach interpretation.

The <finite-friction qualification of a marginal rolling loop> also matters: the contact threshold presumes the stated no-slip idealization. At equality, near the top, required tangential <friction> vanishes linearly with angular distance from the top but $N$ vanishes quadratically. Thus any finite coefficient of <static friction> requires a release height above this limiting bound to maintain rolling everywhere; no coefficient is provided. This does not change the requested ideal contact criterion.

= B9
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B9}

For <collinear inelastic capture followed by two photons>, each particle has $\mathbf p=\gamma m\mathbf u$ and $E=\gamma mc^2$, with $\gamma=(1-u^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$. An isolated collision conserves the vector sum of <momentum> and total <energy>, including <rest energy>, internal excitation and radiation. It need not conserve the sum of individual <rest masses>. At low <speed>,
$$
E=mc^2+\tfrac12mu^2+O(mu^4/c^2),
$$
recovering the classical <kinetic energy> after subtracting $mc^2$.

Here the incoming <Lorentz factors> are $5/3$ and $5/4$, so
$$
E_1=E_2=5mc^2,\qquad p_{1x}=4mc,\quad p_{2x}=-3mc.
$$
The composite therefore has
$$
\boxed{v=\frac{pc^2}{E}=\frac c{10}\text{ in the }+x\text{ direction},\qquad
M=\frac{\sqrt{E^2-p^2c^2}}{c^2}=\sqrt{99}\,m=3\sqrt{11}\,m.}
$$
Its <rest mass> exceeds $7m$ because relative <kinetic energy> has become internal <rest energy>.

For the subsequent collinear <photon> pair, $E_++E_-=10mc^2$ and $(E_+-E_-)/c=mc$. Thus $E_+=11mc^2/2$, $E_-=9mc^2/2$, and
$$
\boxed{\nu_+/\nu_-=11/9.}
$$
The positive-$x$ <photon> is the higher-frequency one.

= Section C
{c}
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= C10
{parent=Section C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=C10}

In the <uniform-Earth chord oscillator>, neglect the <mass> removed by the narrow tunnel and any rotation of the <Earth>. Inside a uniform <sphere>, the <shell theorem> gives $\mathbf F=-(4\pi G\rho m/3)\mathbf r$. On the constrained chord $\mathbf r=(d,y)$, its along-tunnel component is
$$
\boxed{F_y=-m\omega_0^2y,\qquad\omega_0^2=\frac{4\pi G\rho}{3}.}
$$
The transverse component is balanced by the tunnel constraint. With release from $y_s=\sqrt{R^2-d^2}$ at rest,
$$
\ddot y+\omega_0^2y=0,\qquad \boxed{y(t)=y_s\cos\omega_0t.}
$$
The far end is $-y_s$, so **$t_{\rm transit}=\pi/\omega_0\approx2530\,\mathrm s=42.2\,\mathrm{min}$**, independent of chord offset in this ideal model. Here $\rho=5515\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and $G=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$ give $\omega_0=1.24171\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$.

With linear drag, $\ddot y+(b/m)\dot y+\omega_0^2y=0$. Inserting a <complex exponential> $e^{-(\gamma+i\omega)t}$ gives
$$
\gamma^2-\omega^2-\frac bm\gamma+\omega_0^2+i\omega(2\gamma-b/m)=0,
$$
so in the <underdamped> regime
$$
\boxed{\gamma=\frac b{2m},\qquad\omega=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\gamma^2}.}
$$
Real physical solutions combine conjugate modes. Release from rest specifically gives $y=y_se^{-\gamma t}[\cos\omega t+(\gamma/\omega)\sin\omega t]$; a lone exponential with prefactor $y_s$ does not impose that initial <velocity>.

The <critical gravity-tunnel settling time> follows from critical damping, the fastest non-oscillatory release:
$$
\boxed{b_c=2m\omega_0,\qquad b_c/m=2.48342\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad
y(t)=y_s(1+\omega_0t)e^{-\omega_0t}.}
$$
Interpret the 1% tolerance relative to the initial displacement from equilibrium. With $s=\omega_0t$, $(1+s)e^{-s}=0.01$ has $s=6.63835$. Hence
$$
\boxed{t_{1\%}=6.63835/\omega_0\approx5346\,\mathrm s=89.1\,\mathrm{min}.}
$$
The equilibrium is reached only asymptotically; using a pure $e^{-\omega_0t}$ would miss the critical-damping polynomial factor and underestimate this time.

= C11
{parent=Section C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=C11}

For the <series-fed parallel LC voltage response>, use <phasors> with time dependence $e^{i\omega t}$. The parallel branch has impedance $iX$, where $X=\omega L/(1-\omega^2LC)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{Z=R+iX,\quad|Z|=\sqrt{R^2+X^2},\quad\arg Z=\operatorname{atan2}(X,R).}
$$
At the ideal parallel <resonance> $\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}$ its input impedance diverges. The input-current phase changes sign on crossing this pole; expressions there are interpreted by limits.

The <capacitor> <voltage> equals the parallel-branch <voltage>. Define $q=R(1-\omega^2LC)/(\omega L)$. Then
$$
\boxed{H(\omega)=\frac{V_C}{V_i}=\frac{iX}{R+iX}=\frac1{1-iq},\quad
|H|=(1+q^2)^{-1/2},\quad\arg H=\arctan q.}
$$
At low <frequency> $H\sim i\omega L/R$; at high <frequency> $H\sim-i/(\omega RC)$; at <resonance> $H=1$. Thus it is a **band-pass <voltage> filter or <resonant frequency> selector**. Its phase decreases continuously from $+\pi/2$ through zero to $-\pi/2$. Half-power points satisfy $|q|=1$ and
$$
\omega_\pm=\frac{\sqrt{(RC)^{-2}+4/(LC)}\pm(RC)^{-1}}2,\qquad
\omega_+-\omega_-=\frac1{RC}.
$$

\Image[paper-1-ia2010-lc-response.png]
{title=Voltage response of a parallel resonator}
{description=Original transfer amplitude and phase for the stated series-fed parallel LC circuit. The example has R sqrt(C/L)=1; resonance and both half-power points are marked, with asymptotic phases shown.}

For the supplied component values and $\omega=2\pi(50)\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $X=421.745\,\Omega$ and $V_{C,\rm pk}=99.3046\,\mathrm V$. The element peak currents are
$$
\boxed{I_{R,\rm pk}=\frac{100}{\sqrt{50^2+421.745^2}}=0.23546\,\mathrm A,\quad
I_{L,\rm pk}=\frac{99.3046}{\omega(0.150)}=2.10731\,\mathrm A,\quad
I_{C,\rm pk}=\omega(60\times10^{-6})(99.3046)=1.87185\,\mathrm A.}
$$
<Inductor> and <capacitor> currents are opposite <phasors>; their magnitudes subtract here rather than add. The <resistor> carries the input current, with
$$
\boxed{I_{\rm rms}=I_{R,\rm pk}/\sqrt2=0.16650\,\mathrm A.}
$$
These results assume ideal components and sinusoidal <steady state>.

= Section D
{c}
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= D12
{parent=Section D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D12}

The displacement is along $x$, transverse to propagation in the membrane's $yz$ plane. Write $A=|A|e^{i\phi}$; then $d=|A|\cos(\omega t-k_yy-k_zz+\phi)$. Here $|A|$ is the displacement <amplitude>, $\phi$ the initial phase, $\omega$ the <angular frequency> and $\mathbf k$ the <wavevector> normal to constant-phase lines. Its magnitude is $2\pi/\lambda$, and its direction specifies phase propagation for the chosen sign convention.

For <equal-phase two-wave membrane modes>, two equal <complex amplitudes> with opposite $k_y$ sum to
$$
d=2\operatorname{Re}\{A\cos(k_yy)e^{i(\omega t-k_zz)}\}.
$$
The edge condition gives $\cos(k_ya/2)=0$, hence $k_y=(2j+1)\pi/a$, $j=0,1,\ldots$, for this even-in-$y$ family. Opposite relative phase gives the <sine> family; together they yield all Dirichlet transverse modes $k_y=n\pi/a$, $n\ge1$. For the equal-phase family,
$$
\boxed{k_z=\sqrt{(\omega/c)^2-[(2j+1)\pi/a]^2}.}
$$
The lowest mode has $k_y=\pi/a$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{v_z=\frac\omega{k_z}=\frac c{\sqrt{1-(\lambda/2a)^2}},\qquad0<\lambda<2a.}
$$
It approaches $c$ for $\lambda\to0$ and diverges as $\lambda\to2a^-$; there is no real travelling-mode $k_z$ beyond the cutoff. This is an axial <phase velocity>, not the <group velocity>: $v_g=c\sqrt{1-(\lambda/2a)^2}<c$. Here $c$ denotes the membrane wave <speed>, not the <speed of light>.

\Image[paper-1-ia2010-membrane.png]
{title=Membrane cutoff and evanescent response}
{description=Original lowest-mode phase-velocity curve, with group velocity distinguished, and a schematic below-cutoff displacement envelope of exponential form. All lengths and amplitudes are dimensionless choices; no source apparatus drawing is copied.}

For the <evanescent membrane response below cutoff>, below $f_c=c/(2a)$, put $k_z=-i\kappa$ for decay along positive $z$, with $\kappa=\sqrt{(\pi/a)^2-(\omega/c)^2}$. A localized central drive creates an evanescent, sinusoidally oscillating deformation decaying away in both axial directions. Far enough from the drive the leading profile is proportional to $\cos(\pi y/a)e^{-\kappa|z-z_0|}\cos(\omega t+\phi_0)$. Higher evanescent modes contribute near the point source. The pattern has no steadily advancing axial phase front or far-field propagating wave; decreasing <frequency> increases its spatial localization.

= D13
{parent=Section D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D13}

Direct differentiation gives $\psi''/\psi=x^2/a^4-1/a^2$. Inserting this into the <Schrödinger equation> and separately matching the $x^2$ and constant terms yields
$$
\boxed{a^4=\frac{\hbar^2}{2mV_0},\qquad a^2=\frac{\hbar}{\sqrt{2mV_0}},\qquad E=\frac{\hbar^2}{2ma^2}.}
$$
For $V_0=m\omega_0^2/2$, **$a^2=\hbar/(m\omega_0)$ and $E=\hbar\omega_0/2$**. The <normalizable> <Gaussian> has no <nodes>, as expected for the nondegenerate one-dimensional <ground state> of this confining potential.

The normalization integral is $|C|^2a\sqrt\pi=1$. Choose its irrelevant <global phase> so that
$$
\boxed{C=(\pi a^2)^{-1/4},\qquad dP=\frac1{\sqrt\pi a}e^{-x^2/a^2}\,dx.}
$$
For the <oscillator ground-state density in a comparison wavelength>, choose a free comparison particle with the same total <energy>. It has $p=\sqrt{2mE}=\hbar/a$, hence comparison <wavelength> $\lambda=h/p=2\pi a$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{dP=\frac{2\sqrt\pi}{\lambda}\exp\!\left(-\frac{4\pi^2x^2}{\lambda^2}\right)dx.}
$$
The bound density is localized on scale $a=\lambda/(2\pi)$, with <standard deviation> $\lambda/(2\pi\sqrt2)$, rather than being spatially uniform like the density of a single free <plane wave>. The comparison does not assign constant free-particle <momentum> to the <bound state>; its total <energy> also includes <potential energy>.

= Section E
{c}
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= E14
{parent=Section E}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=E14}

The closed surface integral sums the outward electric <flux>: $\mathbf E$ is the <electric field>, $d\mathbf S$ its outward vector <area>, $Q$ the total enclosed charge and $\epsilon_0$ the <vacuum> permittivity. <Gauss's law> relates that <flux> to enclosed charge for any closed surface. High <symmetry> can make the field's magnitude constant on a suitable <Gaussian surface> and fix its direction; without <symmetry>, <flux> alone does not determine the local field.

For an isolated conducting <sphere>, charges reside at its surface and the exterior field is radial. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf E(r)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r}\quad(r>a),\qquad
V(a)=\int_a^\infty E(r)\,dr=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0a},\qquad C=4\pi\epsilon_0a.}
$$
The conductor has zero interior field. Integrating exterior field <energy> gives
$$
U=\int_a^\infty\frac12\epsilon_0\left(\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0r^2}\right)^2 4\pi r^2dr
=\boxed{\frac{Q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0a}}.
$$
Let the initial charge per drop be $q=4\pi\epsilon_0aV_1$. After disconnecting the charging supply, charge is conserved. <Volume> conservation gives final <radius> $A=2^{1/3}a$, total charge $2q$ and
$$
\boxed{V_f=\frac{2q}{4\pi\epsilon_0A}=2^{2/3}V_1.}
$$
Initial mutual <energy> is negligible at the stipulated large separation. Hence
$$
U_i=\frac{q^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0a},\qquad
U_f=\frac{4q^2}{8\pi\epsilon_0\,2^{1/3}a}=2^{2/3}U_i,\qquad
\boxed{U_i/U_f=2^{-2/3}.}
$$
**The <electrostatic energy increase on equal-drop coalescence> is positive.** It can be supplied by the decrease in surface <energy> during coalescence and/or by external mechanical work bringing like charges together. Remaining <energy> can become motion and heat. If the charge is too large, surface-energy release alone need not suffice to make merging spontaneous; conservation does not assert that it will. Keeping the drops connected to a <voltage> supply would instead be a different charge-balance problem.

= E15
{parent=Section E}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=E15}

For <point masses>, Newton's <force> is attractive along their separation with magnitude $Gm_1m_2/r^2$. Treat the narrow rod as uniform, with <linear mass density> $\mu=M/(2a)$; a <mass> profile would otherwise be needed beyond total <mass> and <length>.

For <axial and transverse gravity of a uniform rod>, on the axis at $b>a$, integrating the element <force> gives
$$
\boxed{F_1=Gm\mu\int_{-a}^a\frac{dx}{(b-x)^2}=\frac{GMm}{b^2-a^2},}
$$
directed toward the rod. On the perpendicular bisector, axial components cancel and transverse components add:
$$
\boxed{F_2=Gm\mu r\int_{-a}^a\frac{dx}{(x^2+r^2)^{3/2}}=\frac{GMm}{r\sqrt{r^2+a^2}}.}
$$
At $r=b\gg a$,
$$
F_1=\frac{GMm}{b^2}\left[1+\frac{a^2}{b^2}+O(a^4/b^4)\right],\quad
F_2=\frac{GMm}{b^2}\left[1-\frac{a^2}{2b^2}+O(a^4/b^4)\right].
$$
Both approach the point-mass <force>, but $F_1/F_2=1+3a^2/(2b^2)+O(a^4/b^4)$, so the axial attraction is slightly larger.

For a <circular orbit perpendicular to a uniform rod>, rotational <symmetry> about the rod makes a centred <circular orbit> possible in its perpendicular-bisector plane. With $m\ll M$, centrifugal balance gives
$$
\boxed{v(r)=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{\sqrt{r^2+a^2}}}
=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{a}}[1+(r/a)^2]^{-1/4}.}
$$
For $r\gg a$, it is Keplerian, $v\sim\sqrt{GM/r}$. For rod thickness $\ll r\ll a$, the <force> approximates that of an infinite line and **$v\to\sqrt{GM/a}$**, a flat <speed> limit rather than a divergence.

\Image[paper-1-ia2010-rod-orbit.png]
{title=Circular speed around a uniform rod}
{description=Original orbital speed normalized by sqrt(GM/a), against perpendicular orbit radius divided by the rod half-length. The small-radius plateau and large-radius Kepler asymptote are marked; the zero-thickness rod approximation requires orbit radius larger than the physical rod thickness.}
